WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.682
Welcome to Module 4 of the
Attribute-Driven Migration learning path:

2
00:00:04.682 --> 00:00:06.388
move Group Policy as code.

3
00:00:06.388 --> 00:00:10.792
This module serves three of the
implementation guide's gates, 0,

4
00:00:10.792 --> 00:00:15.788
5 and 8, and takes Chapter 06 of
Building the Attribute-Driven Migration.

5
00:00:15.788 --> 00:00:20.550
You'll read Group Policy analytics
into a move plan, one row per setting.

6
00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:24.179
You'll keep each settings catalog
policy as a reviewed file,

7
00:00:24.179 --> 00:00:27.438
with an owner and a decision
record for every setting.

8
00:00:27.438 --> 00:00:30.397
And you'll run a sync that
reads the tenant first,

9
00:00:30.397 --> 00:00:32.901
and creates only what its dry run showed.

10
00:00:32.901 --> 00:00:35.013
Then you'll run it all on your laptop,

11
00:00:35.013 --> 00:00:38.875
against a fake Graph and a fixture
tenant, and break it on purpose.

12
00:00:38.875 --> 00:00:44.630
The code is illustrative: tested against
fixtures, not against a production tenant.

13
00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:49.692
Why does this need code?

14
00:00:49.692 --> 00:00:53.429
The organizational unit (OU)
tree scoped Group Policy,

15
00:00:53.429 --> 00:00:56.656
and Group Policy
inheritance retires with it.

16
00:00:56.656 --> 00:01:00.405
So every GPO setting has to
land somewhere on purpose.

17
00:01:00.405 --> 00:01:06.483
Gate 0 splits every GPO by track,
and its Validate step runs VK-INT-07,

18
00:01:06.483 --> 00:01:13.555
to show which GPOs Group Policy analytics
has imported and how ready they are.

19
00:01:13.555 --> 00:01:17.093
Gate 5 moves each setting
to exactly one owner.

20
00:01:17.093 --> 00:01:21.692
And Gate 8 cuts over by disabling
GPO links, never deleting them.

21
00:01:21.692 --> 00:01:26.467
Moving Group Policy to Intune
changes the baseline of every device.

22
00:01:26.467 --> 00:01:30.366
This module is the code between
those gates: a plan per setting,

23
00:01:30.366 --> 00:01:35.550
a library of reviewed files, and a sync
that creates only what a dry run showed.

24
00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.111
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

25
00:01:42.111 --> 00:01:44.475
which takes about fifty
minutes with the lab.

26
00:01:44.475 --> 00:01:48.315
You'll explain what
VK-INT-07 checks per GPO,

27
00:01:48.315 --> 00:01:51.825
and what the move plan adds per setting.

28
00:01:51.825 --> 00:01:55.825
You'll name the plan's dispositions,
and say what happens to a conflict.

29
00:01:55.825 --> 00:01:57.762
You'll describe a library file:

30
00:01:57.762 --> 00:02:01.325
the policy body Graph receives,
and the record around it.

31
00:02:01.325 --> 00:02:05.325
You'll run the dry run and the
apply, and read the sync record.

32
00:02:05.325 --> 00:02:08.263
And you'll explain why a
drift stops the release,

33
00:02:08.263 --> 00:02:10.975
and why the sync never
sends PATCH or DELETE.

34
00:02:10.975 --> 00:02:17.075
Before you start, do Module 2,
for how VK-INT-07 is built.

35
00:02:17.075 --> 00:02:21.713
Microsoft Automation Module
3 is optional background

36
00:02:21.713 --> 00:02:26.150
on the settings catalog and
Intune's v1.0 and beta APIs.

37
00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:31.940
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7.1 or
later and Pester 5, and no tenant.

38
00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:37.428
Start with where the code sits.

39
00:02:37.428 --> 00:02:41.604
The operator steps for retiring
Group Policy are already written,

40
00:02:41.604 --> 00:02:43.578
in this workbook's own guidance.

41
00:02:43.578 --> 00:02:47.973
This code serves two of them:
importing every in-scope GPO into

42
00:02:47.973 --> 00:02:52.541
Group Policy analytics, and creating
the policy library as code.

43
00:02:52.541 --> 00:02:56.115
Two rules shape it, and both
are this workbook's guidance.

44
00:02:56.115 --> 00:02:59.562
One owning policy, and one
owning tool, per setting,

45
00:02:59.562 --> 00:03:02.840
so the library holds only
settings Intune owns.

46
00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:07.230
And build unassigned: the code
creates policies with no assignments,

47
00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:10.002
and assigning them is
a later, reviewed step.

48
00:03:10.002 --> 00:03:12.277
Microsoft Learn sets the stakes.

49
00:03:12.277 --> 00:03:17.202
Its page on importing GPOs into
Group Policy analytics says the tool

50
00:03:17.202 --> 00:03:21.866
is for Windows only, and imports
GPO exports under four megabytes.

51
00:03:21.866 --> 00:03:27.411
Its page on creating a settings catalog
policy from imported GPOs calls the Migrate

52
00:03:27.411 --> 00:03:29.946
step best effort, and says it stops when

53
00:03:29.946 --> 00:03:32.877
two GPOs set one setting
to different values.

54
00:03:32.877 --> 00:03:36.160
And Learn's Microsoft Graph
reference documents the

55
00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:40.400
migration reports and settings
catalog policies in beta only.

56
00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:49.738
Microsoft Learn's Intune migration guide
points to Microsoft's Intune Graph samples,

57
00:03:49.738 --> 00:03:52.887
on GitHub, for exporting
and importing policies,

58
00:03:52.887 --> 00:03:56.350
and says the scripts change
your tenant and come as-is.

59
00:03:56.350 --> 00:03:59.375
Their settings catalog
folder has an import script.

60
00:03:59.375 --> 00:04:01.964
The repository keeps a pinned copy,

61
00:04:01.964 --> 00:04:06.250
and a test harness runs it,
unmodified, against a fake Graph.

62
00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:07.637
Here's what that shows.

63
00:04:07.637 --> 00:04:11.388
The import creates the policy
with one POST, unassigned.

64
00:04:11.388 --> 00:04:12.763
That's kept as the model.

65
00:04:12.763 --> 00:04:16.673
But run it twice and it creates a
second policy with the same name,

66
00:04:16.673 --> 00:04:18.738
because it never reads the tenant.

67
00:04:18.738 --> 00:04:23.442
It sends to the worldwide Graph host
whatever cloud you signed in to, and Learn's

68
00:04:23.442 --> 00:04:28.363
page on Microsoft Graph national cloud
deployments gives each cloud its own host.

69
00:04:28.363 --> 00:04:31.888
It uses whatever session you
opened, whatever its scopes.

70
00:04:31.888 --> 00:04:37.363
It silently strips service-set properties,
so the body sent isn't the file reviewed.

71
00:04:37.363 --> 00:04:39.788
And it records nothing beyond the screen.

72
00:04:39.788 --> 00:04:45.102
The example adds a sync that reads the
tenant first, a host per cloud, a read-only

73
00:04:45.102 --> 00:04:50.350
scope for the dry run, refusals instead
of silent edits, and a sync record.

74
00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:55.950
The read side has two parts.

75
00:04:55.950 --> 00:04:59.562
VK-INT-07 is the gate.

76
00:04:59.562 --> 00:05:03.280
The validation kit's check
reads the migration reports,

77
00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:08.693
and fails on any GPO targeted in Active
Directory whose readiness isn't complete,

78
00:05:08.693 --> 00:05:11.763
and on any in-scope GPO
that was never imported.

79
00:05:11.763 --> 00:05:15.200
It works per GPO, and
doesn't read the settings.

80
00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:17.075
The move plan works per setting.

81
00:05:17.075 --> 00:05:19.638
It reads each report's setting mappings,

82
00:05:19.638 --> 00:05:24.763
which Learn's Graph beta reference documents,
and gives every row a disposition.

83
00:05:24.763 --> 00:05:27.062
The dispositions are the kit's design.

84
00:05:27.062 --> 00:05:30.775
A candidate is supported and has
an Intune setting definition,

85
00:05:30.775 --> 00:05:32.988
and a person decides whether it moves.

86
00:05:32.988 --> 00:05:37.425
A conflict is two GPOs setting one
definition to different values:

87
00:05:37.425 --> 00:05:40.813
Learn's page on creating
a settings catalog policy

88
00:05:40.813 --> 00:05:44.201
from imported GPOs says
Migrate stops on that case.

89
00:05:44.201 --> 00:05:46.537
The decision record picks the value.

90
00:05:46.537 --> 00:05:51.250
AppLocker and firewall settings go to
Endpoint security, as that page says.

91
00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:55.679
And, in this workbook's guidance, the
plan decides nothing: Group Policy

92
00:05:55.679 --> 00:06:00.690
analytics finds the landing zone, never
whether a setting is still needed.

93
00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:07.640
Each settings catalog
policy is a JSON file.

94
00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:12.342
The policy part is the body Graph
receives, exactly as reviewed, in the shape

95
00:06:12.342 --> 00:06:14.845
Microsoft Learn's Graph beta reference

96
00:06:14.845 --> 00:06:17.878
documents for creating
a configuration policy.

97
00:06:17.878 --> 00:06:22.923
The rest is the record: who approves a
change, which tool owns the settings,

98
00:06:22.923 --> 00:06:27.716
which GPOs they came from, and the I
D of each setting's decision record.

99
00:06:27.716 --> 00:06:31.103
The linter applies ten rules,
and they're the kit's design.

100
00:06:31.103 --> 00:06:33.102
Four carry this workbook's rules.

101
00:06:33.102 --> 00:06:36.072
GPC oh two: the config
owner must be Intune,

102
00:06:36.072 --> 00:06:40.177
because a setting another tool
owns stays with that tool.

103
00:06:40.177 --> 00:06:43.852
GPC oh six: one setting
definition in one policy,

104
00:06:43.852 --> 00:06:47.715
because two policies on
one setting is a conflict.

105
00:06:47.715 --> 00:06:51.403
GPC oh nine: every setting
has a decision record.

106
00:06:51.403 --> 00:06:53.899
And GPC oh eight, with the move plan:

107
00:06:53.899 --> 00:06:58.178
a setting that isn't a candidate
is shown, with its decision.

108
00:06:58.178 --> 00:07:02.689
GPC ten warns on a value that looks
like a secret: Learn's page on device

109
00:07:02.689 --> 00:07:08.120
profiles says anyone who can read device
configuration can read such values.

110
00:07:11.870 --> 00:07:13.620
The sync is the kit's design.

111
00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:16.575
It reads the tenant's
settings catalog policies,

112
00:07:16.575 --> 00:07:20.342
through the list call Learn's
Graph beta reference documents,

113
00:07:20.342 --> 00:07:24.257
normalizes both sides, and gives
each library policy one action.

114
00:07:24.257 --> 00:07:26.246
Create: it isn't in the tenant.

115
00:07:26.246 --> 00:07:32.195
No change: the same settings, description,
platform, technology and scope tags.

116
00:07:32.195 --> 00:07:36.682
Drift: it differs, and the row
names each setting that changed.

117
00:07:36.682 --> 00:07:40.208
Duplicate: two tenant policies
have the library's name.

118
00:07:40.208 --> 00:07:42.370
Drift and duplicate exit two.

119
00:07:42.370 --> 00:07:45.880
Without the apply option,
the sync is a dry run:

120
00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:49.295
it signs in with read
only, and sends only GET.

121
00:07:49.295 --> 00:07:51.955
With apply, it signs in with read-write,

122
00:07:51.955 --> 00:07:55.558
POSTs each create, reads it
back and compares again.

123
00:07:55.558 --> 00:07:58.849
And it writes nothing at all
if the lint has a finding,

124
00:07:58.849 --> 00:08:01.070
or any policy is drift or duplicate.

125
00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:05.220
It never sends PATCH or DELETE,
although Learn documents both.

126
00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:09.101
A policy that differs from Git
is a decision for a person, and,

127
00:08:09.101 --> 00:08:14.300
in this workbook's guidance, a change
becomes a new version under a new name.

128
00:08:18.050 --> 00:08:18.775
Try it.

129
00:08:18.775 --> 00:08:23.812
Lab 1 runs in PowerShell 7, against
fixtures, and nothing signs in.

130
00:08:23.812 --> 00:08:31.000
Step one starts in the validation kit,
and runs VK-INT-07 on its fail fixture.

131
00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:33.250
One GPO is imported.

132
00:08:33.250 --> 00:08:37.794
Two are targeted in Active Directory
with a readiness of partial or error,

133
00:08:37.794 --> 00:08:40.475
so they hold settings
Intune can't take over.

134
00:08:40.475 --> 00:08:41.737
Those are findings.

135
00:08:41.737 --> 00:08:45.988
An empty GPO is a warning:
confirm where its settings go.

136
00:08:45.988 --> 00:08:49.376
And one in-scope GPO was
never imported at all.

137
00:08:49.376 --> 00:08:54.834
Step two moves to the group policy folder,
and runs Microsoft's import sample twice,

138
00:08:54.834 --> 00:08:57.563
with the same file,
against the fake Graph.

139
00:08:57.563 --> 00:09:00.949
Now the tenant holds error
reporting and dumps twice,

140
00:09:00.949 --> 00:09:02.950
two policies with the same name.

141
00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:05.475
And the calls are two POSTs and no GET.

142
00:09:05.475 --> 00:09:07.310
The sample never looked.

143
00:09:11.060 --> 00:09:15.069
Step three builds the move
plan from the fixture tenant:

144
00:09:15.069 --> 00:09:17.073
eleven rows, from three GPOs.

145
00:09:17.073 --> 00:09:19.957
Five are candidates, such
as disabling Windows

146
00:09:19.957 --> 00:09:22.547
Error Reporting and allowing crash dumps.

147
00:09:22.547 --> 00:09:25.611
Allow hibernate is a
conflict, on two rows,

148
00:09:25.611 --> 00:09:30.747
because the baseline GPO and the
power GPO set it to different values.

149
00:09:30.747 --> 00:09:33.460
The firewall rule goes
to Endpoint security.

150
00:09:33.460 --> 00:09:38.185
Two rows aren't supported, including
a drive map, and one is deprecated.

151
00:09:38.185 --> 00:09:41.061
Notice the user-scope
rows keep their scope:

152
00:09:41.061 --> 00:09:44.097
user settings go to user
groups on the HR track.

153
00:09:44.097 --> 00:09:46.872
Step four lints the
library against that plan.

154
00:09:46.872 --> 00:09:52.199
There's one row, an information item,
GPC oh eight: the hibernate setting

155
00:09:52.199 --> 00:09:57.710
has conflicting GPO values, and decision
D R oh one oh five picks the value.

156
00:09:57.710 --> 00:10:01.235
That's the conflict shown, with
the decision that resolves it.

157
00:10:01.235 --> 00:10:03.730
The output on the slide is trimmed.

158
00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:09.367
Step five runs the sync.

159
00:10:09.367 --> 00:10:14.228
The dry run says create for the error
reporting policy, which isn't in the tenant,

160
00:10:14.228 --> 00:10:17.743
and no change for sign-in and
power, which already matches.

161
00:10:17.743 --> 00:10:21.204
Then the apply says created,
and both exit zero.

162
00:10:21.204 --> 00:10:24.671
The sync record shows the
scope the apply asked for,

163
00:10:24.671 --> 00:10:26.817
read-write, and the I D it created.

164
00:10:26.817 --> 00:10:30.960
Ask for a cloud name the
script doesn't know, U S gov,

165
00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:35.931
and it exits one: the cloud must be
G C C, global, G C C High or D O D.

166
00:10:35.931 --> 00:10:39.142
Step six breaks something on purpose.

167
00:10:39.142 --> 00:10:42.339
Change the hibernate
value in one library file,

168
00:10:42.339 --> 00:10:46.355
and the dry run exits two, with
a drift that names the setting.

169
00:10:46.355 --> 00:10:48.898
Run it with apply, and it still exits two,

170
00:10:48.898 --> 00:10:52.793
and nothing is created, not even
the error reporting policy.

171
00:10:52.793 --> 00:10:54.129
Put the value back.

172
00:10:54.129 --> 00:10:57.405
Step seven runs the tests: forty-six pass.

173
00:10:57.405 --> 00:10:59.910
Step eight deletes the out folder.

174
00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:07.271
Lab 2 runs the same code
against a test tenant with

175
00:11:07.271 --> 00:11:11.322
Intune licenses and a test
GPO export, never production.

176
00:11:11.322 --> 00:11:15.148
Import a test GPO into
Group Policy analytics.

177
00:11:15.148 --> 00:11:22.297
Run VK-INT-07 live, as a read-only
operator, and build the move plan live.

178
00:11:22.297 --> 00:11:26.160
Then dry-run and apply as a
Policy and Profile Manager.

179
00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:30.947
Compare the result with what Group Policy
analytics' own Migrate step builds.

180
00:11:30.947 --> 00:11:34.231
And drill drift: change a
value in the admin center,

181
00:11:34.231 --> 00:11:36.234
and watch the dry run report it.

182
00:11:36.234 --> 00:11:40.735
In G C C High and D O D, pass
the matching cloud option.

183
00:11:40.735 --> 00:11:44.537
To back it out, step eight deletes
the created policies by the

184
00:11:44.537 --> 00:11:48.110
I D's in the sync record, and
the Migrate comparison policy.

185
00:11:48.110 --> 00:11:50.260
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

186
00:11:50.260 --> 00:11:54.050
Follow Chapter 06's Lab 2, step by step.

187
00:11:57.800 --> 00:11:59.187
The federal requirement.

188
00:11:59.187 --> 00:12:04.692
Moving Group Policy to Intune changes the
baseline of every device, so the plan,

189
00:12:04.692 --> 00:12:10.365
the library and the sync record are
evidence for NIST special publication eight

190
00:12:10.365 --> 00:12:14.938
hundred fifty-three's
configuration-management controls: C M two,

191
00:12:14.938 --> 00:12:19.510
baseline configuration, C M three,
configuration change control,

192
00:12:19.510 --> 00:12:23.575
and C M six, configuration settings,
with C A seven and A C six.

193
00:12:23.575 --> 00:12:31.400
VK-INT-07's results show every in-scope
GPO imported and ready, or listed.

194
00:12:31.400 --> 00:12:34.132
The move plan, saved
with the pull request,

195
00:12:34.132 --> 00:12:37.988
gives each setting a disposition
and shows the conflicts.

196
00:12:37.988 --> 00:12:42.750
The library and its decision records give
one owner and one decision per setting.

197
00:12:42.750 --> 00:12:45.750
The sync record shows what
the sync would create,

198
00:12:45.750 --> 00:12:49.275
then wrote only that, with a
read-only scope for the dry run.

199
00:12:49.275 --> 00:12:54.087
A library that differs from the tenant
stops the release, and the run log shows it.

200
00:12:54.087 --> 00:12:57.574
And Microsoft's sample is
pinned and its behavior tested.

201
00:12:57.574 --> 00:13:02.508
The Key Security Indicators come from
FedRAMP's Consolidated Rules for 2026:

202
00:13:02.508 --> 00:13:05.746
they're FedRAMP 20x
indicators for providers,

203
00:13:05.746 --> 00:13:08.675
and the agency lines its
evidence up with them.

204
00:13:08.675 --> 00:13:12.590
Which output supports which
control is this workbook's reading.

205
00:13:16.340 --> 00:13:18.315
Check yourself with two questions.

206
00:13:18.315 --> 00:13:22.690
First: Microsoft's import sample
runs twice with the same file.

207
00:13:22.690 --> 00:13:23.702
What happens?

208
00:13:23.702 --> 00:13:27.657
The answer is b: two policies
with the same name are created,

209
00:13:27.657 --> 00:13:30.540
because the sample never reads the tenant.

210
00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:33.440
You saw the two POSTs
and no GET in step two.

211
00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:36.992
Second: one library file
differs from the tenant's copy,

212
00:13:36.992 --> 00:13:38.965
and you run the sync with apply.

213
00:13:38.965 --> 00:13:40.115
What does it create?

214
00:13:40.115 --> 00:13:42.077
The answer is b: nothing.

215
00:13:42.077 --> 00:13:46.140
It exits two, and writes nothing
while any policy is drift.

216
00:13:46.140 --> 00:13:48.565
Not even the policy
that was marked create.

217
00:13:48.565 --> 00:13:50.315
You saw that in step six.

218
00:13:50.315 --> 00:13:54.505
The module page has two more questions,
on the conflict disposition and

219
00:13:54.505 --> 00:13:58.900
on the dry run's permission, each
linked to the section that answers it.

220
00:14:02.650 --> 00:14:03.475
To sum up.

221
00:14:03.475 --> 00:14:06.777
You read Group Policy analytics
into a plan per setting,

222
00:14:06.777 --> 00:14:11.084
kept the policies as reviewed files,
and saw the sync create only what its

223
00:14:11.084 --> 00:14:15.463
dry run showed, and nothing at all when
the library and the tenant disagree.

224
00:14:15.463 --> 00:14:22.045
The evidence an ISSO keeps: VK-INT-07's
run, the move plan with each pull request,

225
00:14:22.045 --> 00:14:25.806
the library and its
decision records in Git,

226
00:14:25.806 --> 00:14:31.238
and the sync record with its scopes,
file hashes and created I D's.

227
00:14:31.238 --> 00:14:36.862
In Lab 2, add the Intune audit log for the
